WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:08.370
- I want to thank Dr. Morrison

00:00:08.370 --> 00:00:11.193
for his invitation to talk with you today,

00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:15.600
and I was very happy to
share the classes on Tuesday,

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:19.080
which give me a real sense
of what this class is about

00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:20.820
and what it's like,

00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:23.190
and in particular, his comments,

00:00:23.190 --> 00:00:25.770
very, very learned
comments, I want to say.

00:00:25.770 --> 00:00:29.550
I was very impressed with
his grasp of Wittgenstein,

00:00:29.550 --> 00:00:33.270
and about whom I think I know something,

00:00:33.270 --> 00:00:36.540
and also, I was very, very enlightened

00:00:36.540 --> 00:00:39.240
by what he had to say about William James.

00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:42.300
I had just, I've just
come from New Hampshire,

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:44.970
where I spent Thanksgiving,

00:00:44.970 --> 00:00:47.460
and that gave me a sense of the

00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:50.940
where William James spent his last days,

00:00:50.940 --> 00:00:52.443
last years, I think, yeah,

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:56.820
beautiful, beautiful
country you have here.

00:00:56.820 --> 00:00:59.073
Oh my. America's so wonderful.

00:01:01.260 --> 00:01:05.520
Anyway, speaking of Wittgenstein,

00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:08.130
who was this man,

00:01:08.130 --> 00:01:10.953
Maurice O'Connor Drury's teacher,

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:15.220
Wittgenstein wasn't terribly

00:01:16.740 --> 00:01:19.470
impressed by these classes.

00:01:19.470 --> 00:01:22.177
He says, "My lectures are going well.

00:01:22.177 --> 00:01:24.337
"They will never go better,

00:01:24.337 --> 00:01:27.033
"but what effect do they leave behind?

00:01:28.357 --> 00:01:29.733
"Am I helping anybody?

00:01:31.027 --> 00:01:34.503
"What day," his students
learned, "is not worth learning?

00:01:35.587 --> 00:01:37.447
"And the personal impression I make

00:01:37.447 --> 00:01:40.347
"does not serve them with anything."

00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:43.840
That's true for all of them

00:01:44.790 --> 00:01:47.490
with perhaps one or two exceptions,

00:01:47.490 --> 00:01:48.690
so I'm looking around the class now

00:01:48.690 --> 00:01:52.230
to see who the exceptions
are going to be. (chuckles)

00:01:52.230 --> 00:01:53.400
However, I think it's evident

00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:55.503
that at least in Wittgenstein's case,

00:01:56.430 --> 00:01:59.250
in Cambridge University in England,

00:01:59.250 --> 00:02:00.870
far more than one or two students

00:02:00.870 --> 00:02:04.680
were deeply affected by his classes,

00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:08.763
and very prominent among these
was Maurice O'Connor Drury.

00:02:10.710 --> 00:02:12.810
That's a picture of him here.

00:02:12.810 --> 00:02:15.420
I didn't bring all the equipment,

00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:18.900
you know, the bells and whistles with you

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:20.880
because at any lecture I've been

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:23.343
in Ireland, anyway, in
the last couple of years,

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:26.253
the pictures broke down.

00:02:28.140 --> 00:02:31.230
The computers just don't work,

00:02:31.230 --> 00:02:32.670
and in my age group,

00:02:32.670 --> 00:02:36.513
I'm just not enough of a
tech head to control that,

00:02:37.530 --> 00:02:42.180
so this Maurice O'Connor
Drury is a central figure

00:02:42.180 --> 00:02:44.850
that I want to discuss with you today,

00:02:44.850 --> 00:02:48.030
and why Drury, here in this class?

00:02:48.030 --> 00:02:51.870
It's clear that Drury
is relevant to medicine

00:02:51.870 --> 00:02:55.233
and the humanities, HUMS 029.

00:02:56.310 --> 00:03:00.150
Like most figures discussed in this class,

00:03:00.150 --> 00:03:01.380
Drury was a physician.

00:03:01.380 --> 00:03:02.313
He was a doctor,

00:03:03.210 --> 00:03:06.060
but he wrote a highly regarded book

00:03:06.060 --> 00:03:09.873
about medical practice from
a humanistic perspective.

00:03:10.830 --> 00:03:12.600
He was an undergraduate
student of philosophy

00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:14.790
at Cambridge University.

00:03:14.790 --> 00:03:16.410
They didn't call it
philosophy at Cambridge.

00:03:16.410 --> 00:03:18.393
They call it the moral sciences,

00:03:19.230 --> 00:03:21.873
and he was tutored by Wittgenstein.

00:03:23.580 --> 00:03:24.990
Drury had originally intended

00:03:24.990 --> 00:03:27.933
to take orders in the Anglican Church.

00:03:29.340 --> 00:03:33.240
In America here, that's
the Episcopalian community.

00:03:33.240 --> 00:03:34.350
Rejecting that plan,

00:03:34.350 --> 00:03:38.310
he aspired to a career
teaching philosophy,

00:03:38.310 --> 00:03:42.783
but was guided by Wittgenstein
towards medicine instead,

00:03:43.830 --> 00:03:46.500
and Wittgenstein continued
with a mentoring role

00:03:46.500 --> 00:03:49.380
towards Drury's medical education

00:03:49.380 --> 00:03:52.410
in Dublin in the late 1930s

00:03:52.410 --> 00:03:56.970
through his early years
as a doctor in the royal,

00:03:56.970 --> 00:04:01.920
the British Royal Army Medical
Corps during World War II,

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:05.700
and he recommended that after the war,

00:04:05.700 --> 00:04:09.600
Drury should specialize in
psychiatry, which Drury did,

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:12.693
so this man had a huge
influence on his life,

00:04:14.370 --> 00:04:15.603
on Drury's life.

00:04:16.860 --> 00:04:19.470
Despite a very busy professional life,

00:04:19.470 --> 00:04:23.580
Drury persisted in being
deeply involved in philosophy,

00:04:23.580 --> 00:04:24.413
bringing to bear

00:04:24.413 --> 00:04:26.700
what he had learned from
Wittgenstein in particular

00:04:26.700 --> 00:04:30.060
to issues that arose in the
practice of his psychiatry,

00:04:30.060 --> 00:04:32.400
and we're going to talk about that.

00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:34.170
My own involvement in this story

00:04:34.170 --> 00:04:36.213
is that I've assembled his writings,

00:04:37.080 --> 00:04:40.477
previously published and
unpublished, in this book,

00:04:40.477 --> 00:04:44.160
"The Selected Writings of
Maurice O'Connor Drury,"

00:04:44.160 --> 00:04:45.690
and I think I mentioned to you last day,

00:04:45.690 --> 00:04:49.497
it's published by the same
publisher as "Harry Potter,"

00:04:50.730 --> 00:04:54.873
so that's my big claim to fame. (chuckles)

00:04:55.770 --> 00:04:58.030
The collection justifies considering him

00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:01.140
alongside the physician writers

00:05:01.140 --> 00:05:02.853
that are featured in your course,

00:05:04.410 --> 00:05:08.940
Bulgakov, Henry Marsh, Atul Gawande,

00:05:08.940 --> 00:05:13.230
and Lisa Sanders of the
Yale Medical School.

00:05:13.230 --> 00:05:17.130
All have or are deploying perspectives

00:05:17.130 --> 00:05:21.810
informed by the humanities
to enrich medical practice,

00:05:21.810 --> 00:05:23.260
and actually, the last day

00:05:24.900 --> 00:05:28.320
Dr. Morrison asked me to explain

00:05:28.320 --> 00:05:30.150
why the humanities?

00:05:30.150 --> 00:05:34.920
And I think the humanities' role

00:05:34.920 --> 00:05:38.160
is to foster

00:05:38.160 --> 00:05:40.353
or take care of your soul,

00:05:41.730 --> 00:05:43.353
and what do I mean by your soul?

00:05:44.250 --> 00:05:48.993
The Latin for soul is anima,
so it's what animates you,

00:05:49.860 --> 00:05:51.870
what makes you alive

00:05:51.870 --> 00:05:54.660
when you cry, when you smile,

00:05:54.660 --> 00:05:57.870
when you're with friends,
when you're in love.

00:05:57.870 --> 00:05:58.803
It's your soul,

00:06:00.540 --> 00:06:02.760
so we have soul music.

00:06:02.760 --> 00:06:04.830
It's a wonderful word,

00:06:04.830 --> 00:06:08.580
and the humanities foster that.

00:06:08.580 --> 00:06:09.720
That's what they're there.

00:06:09.720 --> 00:06:11.940
You read a novel. You read a poem.

00:06:11.940 --> 00:06:15.663
You listen to music.
You take part in a play.

00:06:17.730 --> 00:06:20.193
You are at play. You dance.

00:06:22.230 --> 00:06:25.320
That's what the humanity, it's your human,

00:06:25.320 --> 00:06:27.000
what you can do as a human being

00:06:27.000 --> 00:06:29.373
and to foster and strengthen that,

00:06:30.720 --> 00:06:34.143
so Drury understood
that humanistic insight,

00:06:35.310 --> 00:06:37.140
valuable for medical practice,

00:06:37.140 --> 00:06:40.050
was not confined to his
subject, philosophy,

00:06:40.050 --> 00:06:41.310
and in doctors,

00:06:41.310 --> 00:06:43.710
and indeed, all of
those who have a concern

00:06:43.710 --> 00:06:47.820
for how medicine is practiced
in civil society, generally,

00:06:47.820 --> 00:06:50.257
could gain from reading,
and I quote from him,

00:06:50.257 --> 00:06:53.467
"The great novelists, like Tolstoy,

00:06:53.467 --> 00:06:57.807
"the dramatists,
biographers, and historians."

00:06:58.980 --> 00:07:01.620
The person who brought
us here this morning

00:07:01.620 --> 00:07:04.710
is about to, is a theater director

00:07:04.710 --> 00:07:07.350
in Rhode Island, Providence,

00:07:07.350 --> 00:07:09.840
and she's just starting next week

00:07:09.840 --> 00:07:13.710
on a play called "The Faith Healers,"

00:07:13.710 --> 00:07:15.480
and I would absolutely love

00:07:15.480 --> 00:07:19.500
to be a kind of gofer with her,

00:07:19.500 --> 00:07:23.490
you know, seeing how a play,
how you put a play together.

00:07:23.490 --> 00:07:24.873
It's extraordinary.

00:07:25.770 --> 00:07:30.600
Now, it seems to me
that this is the thesis

00:07:30.600 --> 00:07:32.700
that underlies this course,

00:07:32.700 --> 00:07:35.700
that medicine is the most...

00:07:35.700 --> 00:07:37.297
Think about this. This is a quotation.

00:07:37.297 --> 00:07:39.490
"The most humanistic of the sciences

00:07:40.897 --> 00:07:45.397
"and the most scientific

00:07:45.397 --> 00:07:46.857
"of the humanities."

00:07:49.260 --> 00:07:50.370
It straddles...

00:07:50.370 --> 00:07:51.993
Medicine, properly practiced,

00:07:52.950 --> 00:07:55.680
is the most humanistic of the sciences

00:07:55.680 --> 00:07:58.443
and the most scientific of the humanities.

00:08:00.930 --> 00:08:02.010
As I've already said,

00:08:02.010 --> 00:08:04.560
the predominant philosophical
influence on Drury

00:08:04.560 --> 00:08:05.793
was Wittgenstein,

00:08:07.860 --> 00:08:10.350
and Wittgenstein is regarded,

00:08:10.350 --> 00:08:13.080
along with Martin Heidegger,

00:08:13.080 --> 00:08:16.560
as the most innovative
philosophers of the 20th century,

00:08:16.560 --> 00:08:18.540
and as I become clear to you,

00:08:18.540 --> 00:08:21.750
Wittgenstein had a profound
interest in medicine,

00:08:21.750 --> 00:08:25.200
and the focus of his interest in medicine

00:08:25.200 --> 00:08:27.183
was Drury and his career,

00:08:28.050 --> 00:08:30.330
but so, also, did Martin Heidegger,

00:08:30.330 --> 00:08:32.850
the German philosopher.

00:08:32.850 --> 00:08:34.750
Philosophy is kind of divided into two

00:08:37.500 --> 00:08:39.330
styles of philosophy,

00:08:39.330 --> 00:08:40.980
the anglophone philosophy,

00:08:40.980 --> 00:08:43.740
which is what you would
probably mainly get here

00:08:43.740 --> 00:08:46.230
in any English-speaking country,

00:08:46.230 --> 00:08:50.070
like England, Ireland,
America, Canada, Australia,

00:08:50.070 --> 00:08:54.273
you know, the former British Empire,

00:08:56.340 --> 00:09:00.240
but Heidegger also had a
huge interest in medicine.

00:09:00.240 --> 00:09:01.533
He was based in Germany,

00:09:02.400 --> 00:09:03.420
and he gave rise

00:09:03.420 --> 00:09:06.480
to what's called
phenomenological philosophy,

00:09:06.480 --> 00:09:09.960
and for 10 years now,

00:09:09.960 --> 00:09:13.470
he really did spoil his copybook
during the Second World War

00:09:13.470 --> 00:09:18.470
because he kind of
collaborated with the Nazis

00:09:19.170 --> 00:09:22.920
in the university system, and he said...

00:09:22.920 --> 00:09:26.130
But he did have this seminar

00:09:26.130 --> 00:09:29.790
in Switzerland after the
war for about 10 years

00:09:29.790 --> 00:09:33.150
called the Zollikon Seminars,

00:09:33.150 --> 00:09:36.060
and it was mainly for doctors

00:09:36.060 --> 00:09:38.913
and mainly psychiatrists and philosophers,

00:09:39.810 --> 00:09:41.820
and one of his quotations just,

00:09:41.820 --> 00:09:43.590
I'm not going to spend
a lot of time with him,

00:09:43.590 --> 00:09:47.017
but he said, "Blood
pressure can be measured,

00:09:47.017 --> 00:09:49.347
"but not life pressures."

00:09:51.360 --> 00:09:52.860
You can measure your blood pressure.

00:09:52.860 --> 00:09:54.063
I can do it on my watch.

00:09:55.080 --> 00:09:56.973
It's probably zooming right now,

00:09:58.320 --> 00:10:01.443
but not your life pressures,
what's really bothering you.

00:10:02.490 --> 00:10:06.030
Heart functioning can be measured,

00:10:06.030 --> 00:10:09.303
but not the loss of heart or heartbreak,

00:10:15.275 --> 00:10:19.770
and he focused on the
patient's experience of disease

00:10:19.770 --> 00:10:22.980
in the form of pain or discomfort,

00:10:22.980 --> 00:10:27.120
emotional distress and depression,

00:10:27.120 --> 00:10:31.140
but that, in itself, is
not anything measurable,

00:10:31.140 --> 00:10:33.893
and, of course, science has
to do with what's measurable.

00:10:34.897 --> 00:10:37.683
"One cannot measure tears.

00:10:39.997 --> 00:10:43.113
"Measures, at best, a fluid,

00:10:44.767 --> 00:10:49.647
"but the drops of the
fluid are not the tears."

00:10:50.880 --> 00:10:53.973
I think it's a beautiful
way of putting it.

00:10:54.810 --> 00:10:56.880
I'm going to move away from Heidegger,

00:10:56.880 --> 00:10:58.170
but we could have another talk

00:10:58.170 --> 00:11:00.420
about the influence of
Heidegger on medicine.

00:11:01.440 --> 00:11:02.460
It's widely accepted

00:11:02.460 --> 00:11:06.360
that a journal kept by Drury as a student,

00:11:06.360 --> 00:11:11.190
later published as "Conversations
With Wittgenstein,"

00:11:11.190 --> 00:11:12.750
has contributed

00:11:12.750 --> 00:11:15.933
the most compelling picture
of Wittgenstein available.

00:11:16.770 --> 00:11:19.650
He was a magnetic personality.

00:11:19.650 --> 00:11:22.050
He attracted interest and
has attracted interest

00:11:22.050 --> 00:11:26.250
far outside the confines
of professional philosophy.

00:11:26.250 --> 00:11:30.157
I don't know if you're
familiar with the film

00:11:30.157 --> 00:11:31.617
"Dancing at Lughnasa,"

00:11:32.490 --> 00:11:35.620
which involves Meryl Streep

00:11:36.780 --> 00:11:38.970
as playing the main thing.

00:11:38.970 --> 00:11:42.810
The author of that play
originally, Brian Friel,

00:11:42.810 --> 00:11:44.647
contacted me once to say,

00:11:44.647 --> 00:11:47.407
"I would like to write a play

00:11:47.407 --> 00:11:50.010
"about Wittgenstein in Ireland,"

00:11:50.010 --> 00:11:51.480
and, of course, I was very excited.

00:11:51.480 --> 00:11:54.450
My 15 moments of fame had arrived,

00:11:54.450 --> 00:11:57.630
and then he invited me
to the famous theater,

00:11:57.630 --> 00:11:59.700
the Abbey Theater in Dublin,

00:11:59.700 --> 00:12:03.900
and everybody was there, from
the president of the country,

00:12:03.900 --> 00:12:08.900
for his new play called
"Give Me Your Answer, Do!"

00:12:09.180 --> 00:12:12.180
I don't know if you
know the little song say

00:12:12.180 --> 00:12:14.493
Daisy, Daisy, give me your answer, do.

00:12:15.420 --> 00:12:17.010
Anyway, that was the name play,

00:12:17.010 --> 00:12:19.440
and I was waiting with bated breath

00:12:19.440 --> 00:12:21.000
to have him talk about, you know,

00:12:21.000 --> 00:12:23.850
have the characters
talk about Wittgenstein,

00:12:23.850 --> 00:12:26.640
but the play turned out
to be about a playwright

00:12:26.640 --> 00:12:29.004
who could not write a
play about Wittgenstein,

00:12:29.004 --> 00:12:32.970
(laughs) so there you are,

00:12:32.970 --> 00:12:34.800
but Wittgenstein's personality

00:12:34.800 --> 00:12:38.880
has attracted a enormous
amount of interest

00:12:38.880 --> 00:12:40.950
outside of philosophy,

00:12:40.950 --> 00:12:44.580
and the standard
biography of Wittgenstein,

00:12:44.580 --> 00:12:46.590
by a man called Ray Monk,

00:12:46.590 --> 00:12:50.097
called "Ludwig Wittgenstein:
The Duty of Genius,"

00:12:51.870 --> 00:12:54.300
has written that it was Drury's journal,

00:12:54.300 --> 00:12:56.400
the "Conversations With Wittgenstein,"

00:12:56.400 --> 00:12:57.690
that first stimulated him

00:12:57.690 --> 00:13:01.080
to imagine a biography of Wittgenstein.

00:13:01.080 --> 00:13:02.160
What struck Monk

00:13:02.160 --> 00:13:06.390
was how Drury's accounts
contrasted so vividly

00:13:06.390 --> 00:13:09.250
with the pale shadow of the real man

00:13:10.620 --> 00:13:13.950
that prevailed in Oxford
in the late 1980s.

00:13:13.950 --> 00:13:18.210
By that time, Wittgenstein
was dead 10, 15 years,

00:13:18.210 --> 00:13:21.870
and Monk was a student of
philosophy at the university,

00:13:21.870 --> 00:13:24.270
and actually, he introduced this book

00:13:24.270 --> 00:13:26.940
and described how, as a student,

00:13:26.940 --> 00:13:29.640
he had gone off to London for the weekend.

00:13:29.640 --> 00:13:31.923
He bought the journals,

00:13:33.665 --> 00:13:36.180
a copy of Wittgenstein's journals.

00:13:36.180 --> 00:13:39.627
He said, "I read it all the way up to the,

00:13:39.627 --> 00:13:43.327
"on the bus back up to Oxford,

00:13:43.327 --> 00:13:46.537
"and at that moment,

00:13:46.537 --> 00:13:49.977
"I decided to write a
biography of Wittgenstein,"

00:13:51.390 --> 00:13:54.480
and this authenticity is found

00:13:54.480 --> 00:13:57.060
not only in Drury's journal,

00:13:57.060 --> 00:13:58.920
but also in essays

00:13:58.920 --> 00:14:01.410
which concentrated on the
philosophical problems

00:14:01.410 --> 00:14:04.080
that arise in psychiatry and psychology,

00:14:04.080 --> 00:14:06.750
which have been collected in Drury's book.

00:14:06.750 --> 00:14:10.710
They were originally
talks to groups of doctors

00:14:10.710 --> 00:14:14.880
in Dublin and Swansea
in England, in Wales,

00:14:14.880 --> 00:14:18.480
and he then produced
"The Danger of Words,"

00:14:18.480 --> 00:14:21.187
and Monk again wrote that,

00:14:21.187 --> 00:14:23.527
"In its tone and its concerns,

00:14:23.527 --> 00:14:26.377
"it was perhaps the most
truly Wittgensteinian work

00:14:26.377 --> 00:14:29.640
"published by any of
Wittgenstein's students."

00:14:29.640 --> 00:14:31.080
With all that in mind,

00:14:31.080 --> 00:14:33.450
I propose to tell you a
little more about Drury

00:14:33.450 --> 00:14:35.430
and his relationship with Wittgenstein,

00:14:35.430 --> 00:14:37.890
a relationship that lasted from 1931,

00:14:37.890 --> 00:14:39.900
when he first met him as a student,

00:14:39.900 --> 00:14:43.563
until Wittgenstein's death
in 1951, over 20 years,

00:14:44.490 --> 00:14:47.550
and now, however, as a
preliminary to that account,

00:14:47.550 --> 00:14:48.510
I think it would be helpful

00:14:48.510 --> 00:14:51.930
to situate the
Wittgenstein/Drury interaction

00:14:51.930 --> 00:14:54.360
as a relatively modern instance

00:14:54.360 --> 00:14:57.870
of an interaction between
Western philosophy and medicine,

00:14:57.870 --> 00:15:00.090
and I say Western philosophy because,

00:15:00.090 --> 00:15:01.440
and Western medicine,

00:15:01.440 --> 00:15:03.813
because there are also
wonderful medicines,

00:15:04.770 --> 00:15:07.320
Chinese medicines and Indian medicines

00:15:07.320 --> 00:15:10.590
and medicines that are
in Indigenous communities

00:15:10.590 --> 00:15:11.423
all over the world.

00:15:11.423 --> 00:15:15.090
I mean, we have just
one version of medicine,

00:15:15.090 --> 00:15:16.020
but it's the one that,

00:15:16.020 --> 00:15:20.550
in this country and my
own country, predominates,

00:15:20.550 --> 00:15:23.550
but this goes back a very long way,

00:15:23.550 --> 00:15:25.380
the interaction between medicine and,

00:15:25.380 --> 00:15:28.683
philosophy and medicine,
back to classical Greece,

00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:33.570
and the famous Christian writer Tertullian

00:15:33.570 --> 00:15:36.810
said it was like a
sisterhood between the two

00:15:36.810 --> 00:15:40.290
because one was a quest
for truth, philosophy,

00:15:40.290 --> 00:15:43.290
and the other was a quest for health,

00:15:43.290 --> 00:15:45.610
but sometimes truth can be understood

00:15:47.430 --> 00:15:51.270
as critical to health,

00:15:51.270 --> 00:15:53.340
mental health in particular,

00:15:53.340 --> 00:15:55.980
so this is why I'm going to bring you

00:15:55.980 --> 00:15:58.500
a passage illustrating all of this

00:15:58.500 --> 00:16:01.200
from one of Plato's dialogues, the Laws,

00:16:01.200 --> 00:16:02.900
and it's in front of you here now.

00:16:04.650 --> 00:16:06.907
He says, "A state's invalides

00:16:06.907 --> 00:16:10.800
"include not only free
men, but slaves too."

00:16:10.800 --> 00:16:12.240
As you know, there was an,

00:16:12.240 --> 00:16:15.510
slaves were an integral part of that,

00:16:15.510 --> 00:16:16.773
of ancient Greece,

00:16:18.630 --> 00:16:20.857
and, "The state's invalides,

00:16:20.857 --> 00:16:24.517
"who are not free, but slaves,

00:16:24.517 --> 00:16:27.967
"are always being treated by other slaves,

00:16:27.967 --> 00:16:31.087
"who either rush about on flying visits

00:16:31.087 --> 00:16:33.747
"or wait to be consulted
in their surgeries."

00:16:34.680 --> 00:16:36.810
Now, apparently, some people speculate

00:16:36.810 --> 00:16:37.950
that this may have been written

00:16:37.950 --> 00:16:42.547
at the time of a plague in Athens.

00:16:42.547 --> 00:16:45.877
"This kind of doctor," the slave doctor,

00:16:45.877 --> 00:16:47.947
"never gives any account

00:16:47.947 --> 00:16:52.597
"of the particular illness
of the individual slave

00:16:52.597 --> 00:16:55.083
"or is prepared to listen to one.

00:16:55.987 --> 00:16:58.507
"He simply prescribes what he thinks best

00:16:58.507 --> 00:17:00.097
"in the light of experience

00:17:00.097 --> 00:17:02.047
"as if he had precise knowledge

00:17:02.047 --> 00:17:04.737
"and with the self-confidence
of a dictator."

00:17:05.940 --> 00:17:07.290
I'm telling you what to do.

00:17:08.797 --> 00:17:12.997
"Then he dashes off on his way
to the next slave's patient,

00:17:12.997 --> 00:17:15.067
"and so takes off his master's shoulders

00:17:15.067 --> 00:17:17.117
"some of the work of attending the sick."

00:17:19.440 --> 00:17:21.177
I don't know if you think this,

00:17:21.177 --> 00:17:23.477
but this is so contemporary
it's unbelievable.

00:17:24.487 --> 00:17:27.037
"The visits of the free
doctor, by contrast,

00:17:27.037 --> 00:17:28.057
"are mostly concerned

00:17:28.057 --> 00:17:31.267
"with treating the illnesses of free men.

00:17:31.267 --> 00:17:35.677
"His method is to construct
an empirical case history

00:17:35.677 --> 00:17:38.887
"by consulting the
invalide and his friends.

00:17:38.887 --> 00:17:42.667
"In this way, he himself
learns something from the sick,

00:17:42.667 --> 00:17:45.187
"and at the same time, he
gives the individual patient

00:17:45.187 --> 00:17:47.403
"all the instructions he can.

00:17:48.397 --> 00:17:49.567
"He gives no prescription

00:17:49.567 --> 00:17:53.433
"until he has somehow gained
the invalide's consent.

00:17:55.087 --> 00:17:58.327
"Then, coaxing him into
continued cooperation,

00:17:58.327 --> 00:18:02.580
"he tries to complete his
restoration to health."

00:18:02.580 --> 00:18:06.540
Now, which of the two methods is best used

00:18:06.540 --> 00:18:09.660
by the physician or
director of bodily regimen,

00:18:09.660 --> 00:18:13.263
of regiment, regimen, I think,
is what it should say there.

00:18:14.490 --> 00:18:16.710
The trainer, the physical trainer,

00:18:16.710 --> 00:18:21.600
was very important in ancient Greece,

00:18:21.600 --> 00:18:22.983
as important as the doctor,

00:18:25.080 --> 00:18:27.420
so the, you know, the man who,

00:18:27.420 --> 00:18:29.313
for example, like a physiotherapist,

00:18:30.300 --> 00:18:32.250
but somebody who kind of,

00:18:32.250 --> 00:18:35.520
if you go to a gym and work, you know,

00:18:35.520 --> 00:18:38.013
helps you to work yourself out, work out.

00:18:39.630 --> 00:18:42.127
He said, "That which
affects the same result

00:18:42.127 --> 00:18:44.257
"by a twofold process

00:18:44.257 --> 00:18:47.017
"or that which implies a single process,

00:18:47.017 --> 00:18:48.037
"the worst of the two,

00:18:48.037 --> 00:18:49.500
"and exasperates the subject."

00:18:49.500 --> 00:18:50.560
What that means is

00:18:51.510 --> 00:18:54.270
the guy who just hands out prescriptions,

00:18:54.270 --> 00:18:55.650
that's one method.

00:18:55.650 --> 00:18:57.300
The guy who listens to his patient

00:18:57.300 --> 00:19:00.330
and examines him
correctly, that's another,

00:19:00.330 --> 00:19:01.743
and you need to do both.

00:19:02.940 --> 00:19:04.740
Now, as the French say,

00:19:04.740 --> 00:19:07.263
plus ça change, plus c'est la même chose.

00:19:08.407 --> 00:19:11.910
Is it not still the case
that far too frequently,

00:19:11.910 --> 00:19:16.110
cursory, hasty, and therefore
not thorough or detailed

00:19:16.110 --> 00:19:20.220
or respectful medical care
is dispensed to the poor

00:19:20.220 --> 00:19:22.440
while the economically endowed

00:19:22.440 --> 00:19:25.350
demand and get considered,

00:19:25.350 --> 00:19:28.590
considerate and considered care,

00:19:28.590 --> 00:19:31.410
and this situation raises
the philosophical question

00:19:31.410 --> 00:19:33.570
that motivated all of Wittgenstein's,

00:19:33.570 --> 00:19:36.930
all of, excuse me, Plato's investigations.

00:19:36.930 --> 00:19:38.970
Can that state of affairs

00:19:38.970 --> 00:19:43.233
be qualified as socially just?

00:19:44.550 --> 00:19:47.490
While it is true that Plato did not ask,

00:19:47.490 --> 00:19:48.840
which he should have done,

00:19:48.840 --> 00:19:52.200
the question about the
institution of slavery,

00:19:52.200 --> 00:19:54.510
I don't think there is more an up-to-date

00:19:54.510 --> 00:19:58.710
or more fundamental question
to be asked of society now,

00:19:58.710 --> 00:20:01.350
and I base this on
talking a lot to doctors,

00:20:01.350 --> 00:20:03.720
to nurses, to occupational therapists,

00:20:03.720 --> 00:20:06.570
to physiotherapists, all sorts of people.

00:20:06.570 --> 00:20:08.940
The medical system, certainly in Ireland,

00:20:08.940 --> 00:20:13.443
and also in England, is
under tremendous pressure,

00:20:14.640 --> 00:20:17.700
and the people who get
the worst end of the stick

00:20:17.700 --> 00:20:20.150
are, of course, the
economically deprived people,

00:20:21.180 --> 00:20:23.070
and indeed, as I mentioned last day,

00:20:23.070 --> 00:20:27.270
an influential group of philosophers,

00:20:27.270 --> 00:20:28.263
all female,

00:20:30.000 --> 00:20:33.660
argued in Oxford for
the continued importance

00:20:33.660 --> 00:20:37.230
of values in running society,

00:20:37.230 --> 00:20:40.353
especially the value of
justice and fairness,

00:20:43.230 --> 00:20:47.850
so I just, I think I've talked
about them the last day,

00:20:47.850 --> 00:20:49.320
but they have stayed,

00:20:49.320 --> 00:20:54.320
they succeeded to introduce a new form of,

00:20:54.690 --> 00:20:56.793
a new take on ethics,

00:20:58.110 --> 00:21:01.410
which argued against the idea that

00:21:01.410 --> 00:21:03.660
if it feels good for me, well, it's okay.

00:21:03.660 --> 00:21:05.100
That's it. That's all.

00:21:05.100 --> 00:21:06.780
That's the end of it.

00:21:06.780 --> 00:21:09.273
If I feel good about it, okay.

00:21:11.400 --> 00:21:12.810
What they were saying is

00:21:12.810 --> 00:21:17.070
it has to be based on certain values,

00:21:17.070 --> 00:21:21.570
behaviors that contribute
to your own goodness,

00:21:21.570 --> 00:21:23.040
but also to those of others.

00:21:23.040 --> 00:21:28.040
We do not live as isolated automatons.

00:21:28.200 --> 00:21:31.680
We live in a social situation,

00:21:31.680 --> 00:21:36.213
and in that, we find our fulfillment.

00:21:39.240 --> 00:21:41.400
Now, you may query how it is

00:21:41.400 --> 00:21:46.290
that philosophers still draw
inspiration from ancient texts,

00:21:46.290 --> 00:21:48.030
from, like, Plato's dialogues,

00:21:48.030 --> 00:21:50.580
which I've just been discussing with you,

00:21:50.580 --> 00:21:54.060
and indeed, what started
out as Aristotle's notes

00:21:54.060 --> 00:21:57.033
about which these female
philosophers based themselves,

00:21:58.170 --> 00:22:01.983
and Drury discussed this practice
explicitly in his letters,

00:22:03.030 --> 00:22:05.640
and he found his answer

00:22:05.640 --> 00:22:08.820
in the perennial nature of philosophy,

00:22:08.820 --> 00:22:13.680
and I've got the, he
found the point elaborated

00:22:13.680 --> 00:22:16.920
in another one of Plato's
dialogues, the Republic,

00:22:16.920 --> 00:22:18.307
and here you see it here.

00:22:18.307 --> 00:22:21.303
"Dialectic philosophical interrogation,

00:22:22.147 --> 00:22:24.937
"in fact, is the only activity

00:22:24.937 --> 00:22:28.807
"whose method is to
challenge its own assumptions

00:22:28.807 --> 00:22:33.067
"so that it may rest
firmly on first principles.

00:22:33.067 --> 00:22:34.147
"Where the eye of the mind

00:22:34.147 --> 00:22:37.927
"gets really bogged down
in a morass of ignorance,

00:22:37.927 --> 00:22:41.667
"dialectic gently pulls
it out and leads it,"

00:22:42.750 --> 00:22:45.727
so the Socratic method
of asking questions,

00:22:45.727 --> 00:22:46.677
"What do you mean?"

00:22:48.750 --> 00:22:51.390
And then you give an answer,

00:22:51.390 --> 00:22:54.088
and they say, "What do you mean by that?

00:22:54.088 --> 00:22:57.570
"And what do you mean
by meaning?" (laughs)

00:22:57.570 --> 00:22:59.910
The contribution of philosophy
is actually the same

00:22:59.910 --> 00:23:00.930
in every generation.

00:23:00.930 --> 00:23:05.070
That's why the answers
that came before, you know,

00:23:05.070 --> 00:23:06.183
are still relevant,

00:23:07.902 --> 00:23:10.800
and it is to foster an
individual's interrogation,

00:23:10.800 --> 00:23:12.550
and that includes every one of you,

00:23:13.740 --> 00:23:18.540
of your own, and in particular,
our cultural assumptions.

00:23:18.540 --> 00:23:20.970
By that I mean the
assumptions that come from us

00:23:20.970 --> 00:23:23.283
that we come almost
with our mother's milk,

00:23:24.330 --> 00:23:26.920
the kind of things that are assumed

00:23:28.020 --> 00:23:29.223
in your family,

00:23:30.360 --> 00:23:33.543
in your high school, in your university,

00:23:34.920 --> 00:23:37.980
and we normally are not aware of that

00:23:37.980 --> 00:23:40.023
unless we interrogate it,

00:23:41.010 --> 00:23:42.570
and Drury believed

00:23:42.570 --> 00:23:45.870
that a medical education
and practice, for example,

00:23:45.870 --> 00:23:47.310
is not exhausted

00:23:47.310 --> 00:23:50.370
by the accumulation of
information and experience,

00:23:50.370 --> 00:23:51.450
particularly information.

00:23:51.450 --> 00:23:53.130
It's not a question of learning,

00:23:53.130 --> 00:23:54.600
learning about every drug

00:23:54.600 --> 00:23:57.960
and every, you know, bodily organ

00:23:57.960 --> 00:23:59.940
and about the interaction thereof.

00:23:59.940 --> 00:24:01.563
It requires something more:

00:24:02.850 --> 00:24:05.670
the willingness to
question medical practice

00:24:05.670 --> 00:24:09.630
in terms of the values
espoused in its performance.

00:24:09.630 --> 00:24:12.270
In other words, to ask questions

00:24:12.270 --> 00:24:15.040
about the questions that medicine asks

00:24:16.230 --> 00:24:18.750
because a doctor coming into

00:24:18.750 --> 00:24:21.370
his consulting rooms

00:24:22.320 --> 00:24:27.030
brings with it the kind of a suite

00:24:27.030 --> 00:24:29.250
of assumptions and questions,

00:24:29.250 --> 00:24:31.550
but maybe he's not asking
the right questions.

00:24:32.460 --> 00:24:34.710
I once asked, I have an uncle.

00:24:34.710 --> 00:24:36.603
He's now dead, but he was a farmer,

00:24:37.680 --> 00:24:40.473
and I used to spend a lot of
time with him on the farm,

00:24:41.400 --> 00:24:44.013
and much later, I asked him,

00:24:45.037 --> 00:24:47.880
"Do you think I would
have made a good farmer?"

00:24:47.880 --> 00:24:51.780
And he said, "No," and I said, "Why?"

00:24:51.780 --> 00:24:52.613
I said, "Why?"

00:24:52.613 --> 00:24:55.717
And he said, "You were
asking the wrong questions

00:24:55.717 --> 00:24:58.380
"when you were aged five." (chuckles)

00:24:58.380 --> 00:24:59.460
You have to,

00:24:59.460 --> 00:25:01.650
you have to ask the right
questions to be a good farmer.

00:25:01.650 --> 00:25:06.090
You have to ask the right
questions to be a good doctor,

00:25:06.090 --> 00:25:07.890
and to ask these questions

00:25:07.890 --> 00:25:11.673
is a philosophical interrogation.

00:25:12.840 --> 00:25:15.420
Now, you will notice that in
the excerpt from the Laws,

00:25:15.420 --> 00:25:18.693
which we read about the state's invalides,

00:25:20.940 --> 00:25:23.790
Plato commends the free man's doctor,

00:25:23.790 --> 00:25:27.360
who constructs an empirical case history

00:25:27.360 --> 00:25:30.780
by consulting the
invalide and his friends.

00:25:30.780 --> 00:25:34.440
In this way, he himself learns
something from the sick,

00:25:34.440 --> 00:25:35.310
and at the same time,

00:25:35.310 --> 00:25:38.853
he gives the individual patient
all the instruction he can.

00:25:39.690 --> 00:25:41.580
In commending this approach,

00:25:41.580 --> 00:25:43.920
Plato the philosopher is referencing

00:25:43.920 --> 00:25:47.580
a fundamental medical institution:

00:25:47.580 --> 00:25:49.320
the clinical interview,

00:25:49.320 --> 00:25:53.283
the formal conversation
between doctor and patients,

00:25:54.150 --> 00:25:56.130
and evidence of its Greek origins

00:25:56.130 --> 00:25:59.100
can be found in the
very words we still use

00:25:59.100 --> 00:26:01.680
to describe the phases of that interview.

00:26:01.680 --> 00:26:03.080
You have that down here now.

00:26:03.990 --> 00:26:06.060
Taking a history.

00:26:06.060 --> 00:26:08.373
The Greek for that is anamnesis,

00:26:09.390 --> 00:26:13.440
which is the very medical
practice singled out by Plato

00:26:13.440 --> 00:26:16.950
as characteristic of and
worthy of a free man,

00:26:16.950 --> 00:26:21.840
attentive listening to the
patient's story/narrative.

00:26:21.840 --> 00:26:24.210
I note the emphasis placed on this phase

00:26:24.210 --> 00:26:25.950
by Gawande and Sanders,

00:26:25.950 --> 00:26:27.780
and it appears in Drury too,

00:26:27.780 --> 00:26:29.760
and indeed, it is the case

00:26:29.760 --> 00:26:31.620
that the dialogue between
doctor and patient

00:26:31.620 --> 00:26:35.160
plays an especially central healing role

00:26:35.160 --> 00:26:37.293
in Drury's specialty, psychiatry,

00:26:38.340 --> 00:26:40.090
much more important than the pills.

00:26:41.610 --> 00:26:43.750
The second is an examination

00:26:45.600 --> 00:26:48.270
using, in addition to hearing,

00:26:48.270 --> 00:26:52.743
the senses of sight and touch,

00:26:54.330 --> 00:26:55.163
so you go on the couch,

00:26:55.163 --> 00:26:59.960
and he uses his fingers, his hands

00:27:03.097 --> 00:27:07.950
to help him to construct
an empirical case history.

00:27:07.950 --> 00:27:09.990
Greek medicine, at its apogee,

00:27:09.990 --> 00:27:14.760
which best in the Greek
doctor Galen of Pergamon,

00:27:14.760 --> 00:27:17.850
which is Turkey, he practiced in Rome,

00:27:17.850 --> 00:27:21.000
was characterized by the
recording of clinical histories

00:27:21.000 --> 00:27:22.980
for comparison's sake.

00:27:22.980 --> 00:27:26.910
Later on, with the invention
of the stethoscope in 1826,

00:27:26.910 --> 00:27:30.330
the range of such examinations
are considerably increased,

00:27:30.330 --> 00:27:33.180
and, of course, is now later augmented

00:27:33.180 --> 00:27:35.490
by comprehensive laboratory tests

00:27:35.490 --> 00:27:38.310
to the extent, however, as Gawande claims,

00:27:38.310 --> 00:27:41.850
that testing has
overwhelmed medical systems.

00:27:41.850 --> 00:27:43.000
It's all about testing.

00:27:43.890 --> 00:27:46.590
Thirdly, in the medical interviews,

00:27:46.590 --> 00:27:49.770
a diagnosis using anatomical

00:27:49.770 --> 00:27:51.390
and with the development of history,

00:27:51.390 --> 00:27:53.370
of medicine, physiology,

00:27:53.370 --> 00:27:56.940
physiological and pathological models.

00:27:56.940 --> 00:28:01.470
Modern medicine relied on such
medicines can be data to 1858

00:28:01.470 --> 00:28:06.390
with the publication of
the book by Rudolf Virchow

00:28:06.390 --> 00:28:09.120
called "Cellular Pathology,"

00:28:09.120 --> 00:28:11.977
However, I note Professor Sanders' view,

00:28:11.977 --> 00:28:14.257
"I saw that what I thought of as diagnosis

00:28:14.257 --> 00:28:16.387
"was wrong much of the time

00:28:16.387 --> 00:28:19.087
"or for the most exciting
parts, in any case.

00:28:19.087 --> 00:28:22.387
"I found that diagnosis was
not the multiplication tables

00:28:22.387 --> 00:28:24.667
"or the application of an algorithm,

00:28:24.667 --> 00:28:27.240
"but more like Sherlock Holmes."

00:28:27.240 --> 00:28:31.470
This insight faces us with
the presence of uncertainty

00:28:31.470 --> 00:28:33.600
in medical investigation,

00:28:33.600 --> 00:28:35.190
which means that it can never

00:28:35.190 --> 00:28:37.980
be simply applying an algorithm.

00:28:37.980 --> 00:28:39.450
That view is reinforced

00:28:39.450 --> 00:28:44.100
by similar insights into
scientific investigation itself,

00:28:44.100 --> 00:28:47.370
even stretching to the
level of quantum physics,

00:28:47.370 --> 00:28:51.063
and I think I mentioned a
book last day that does that.

00:28:52.080 --> 00:28:56.700
Fourthly, there's a prognosis,
again, a Greek word,

00:28:56.700 --> 00:28:59.130
and a preferred method of treatment.

00:28:59.130 --> 00:29:00.750
The goal of the clinical interview

00:29:00.750 --> 00:29:02.310
is to arrive at a medical procedure

00:29:02.310 --> 00:29:05.580
that will lead the embodied being,

00:29:05.580 --> 00:29:09.090
very important in philosophy,
the embodied being,

00:29:09.090 --> 00:29:11.703
not a defective machine,

00:29:14.460 --> 00:29:16.810
and that's, of course,
where the problem comes.

00:29:17.760 --> 00:29:20.370
We can understand machines

00:29:20.370 --> 00:29:23.223
much better than we can
understand human beings.

00:29:24.240 --> 00:29:26.580
To be restored to health,

00:29:26.580 --> 00:29:28.800
the World Health
Organization described that

00:29:28.800 --> 00:29:30.360
as a state of complete

00:29:30.360 --> 00:29:32.820
physical, mental, and social wellbeing

00:29:32.820 --> 00:29:37.170
and not merely the absence
of disease or infirmity.

00:29:37.170 --> 00:29:40.200
Now, with that context sketched,

00:29:40.200 --> 00:29:42.870
I want to see how I'm doing for time.

00:29:42.870 --> 00:29:46.140
I am now in a position to relate
more of Drury's biography,

00:29:46.140 --> 00:29:47.460
highlighting, in particular,

00:29:47.460 --> 00:29:50.730
a letter where Wittgenstein
instructs Drury,

00:29:50.730 --> 00:29:52.200
now a medical student,

00:29:52.200 --> 00:29:57.000
along lines which are strikingly congruent

00:29:57.000 --> 00:30:00.663
with Plato's critique of
current medical practice.

00:30:01.650 --> 00:30:06.650
Drury was born in Wilshire in UK in 1907,

00:30:06.720 --> 00:30:09.420
and, as I said,

00:30:09.420 --> 00:30:14.420
both his parents were Irish-born,
but Anglican Christians,

00:30:14.700 --> 00:30:17.880
that's to say members of
the Church of England,

00:30:17.880 --> 00:30:21.930
and after his retirement
to Exeter in Devon,

00:30:21.930 --> 00:30:24.990
Con Drury went to the
grammar school there.

00:30:24.990 --> 00:30:27.273
While still at that high school,

00:30:29.070 --> 00:30:33.213
that's the modern equivalent
of a grammar school,

00:30:34.170 --> 00:30:37.230
he went up to Trinity
College in Cambridge in 1926

00:30:37.230 --> 00:30:39.150
to study what was then called

00:30:39.150 --> 00:30:42.510
the moral sciences, as I
mentioned, that's philosophy,

00:30:42.510 --> 00:30:45.810
with a long-term plan
to take Anglican orders.

00:30:45.810 --> 00:30:50.810
In Drury's third year at
Trinity College in Cambridge,

00:30:51.180 --> 00:30:53.190
Ludwig Wittgenstein returned to Cambridge,

00:30:53.190 --> 00:30:55.110
where he had studied
under Bertrand Russell,

00:30:55.110 --> 00:30:57.180
that's all mentioned the last day,

00:30:57.180 --> 00:31:00.030
for some years before the First World War

00:31:00.030 --> 00:31:01.203
to teach philosophy.

00:31:02.520 --> 00:31:05.520
He was immediately fell
under Wittgenstein's spell,

00:31:05.520 --> 00:31:06.960
and thereafter,

00:31:06.960 --> 00:31:09.360
almost all the vocational
decisions in his life

00:31:09.360 --> 00:31:13.473
were strongly influenced, if
not dominated by his teacher.

00:31:14.400 --> 00:31:17.310
Hence, although he resisted
Wittgenstein's counsel

00:31:17.310 --> 00:31:19.320
to abandon his plan to enroll

00:31:19.320 --> 00:31:21.870
in the Anglican theological
college at Cambridge,

00:31:21.870 --> 00:31:23.103
Westcott House,

00:31:24.480 --> 00:31:26.257
because Wittgenstein claimed,

00:31:26.257 --> 00:31:29.067
"That clerical collar would choke you."

00:31:31.560 --> 00:31:35.340
Drury left the seminary
after a year, in fact,

00:31:35.340 --> 00:31:37.650
and then he followed Wittgenstein's advice

00:31:37.650 --> 00:31:39.213
to live among ordinary people.

00:31:42.330 --> 00:31:43.980
Wittgenstein was very strongly of the...

00:31:43.980 --> 00:31:45.780
Wittgenstein himself had come from

00:31:45.780 --> 00:31:48.843
probably the richest family in Austria,

00:31:49.710 --> 00:31:52.530
and he gave away all his,

00:31:52.530 --> 00:31:54.270
after returning from the First World War,

00:31:54.270 --> 00:31:58.170
he gave away all his money
to his brothers and sisters,

00:31:58.170 --> 00:32:02.700
and thereafter, he lived
very, very frugally,

00:32:02.700 --> 00:32:04.563
and you have mentioned last day

00:32:04.563 --> 00:32:07.860
that the number of different
kind of things he tried,

00:32:07.860 --> 00:32:11.460
school teaching, gardening,

00:32:11.460 --> 00:32:13.833
architecture, God knows,

00:32:15.900 --> 00:32:19.050
but anyway, in Drury's case,
he was involved in schemes

00:32:19.050 --> 00:32:21.600
to assist the vast number of
unemployed people in Britain

00:32:21.600 --> 00:32:25.140
following the worldwide economic
depression of the 1930s,

00:32:25.140 --> 00:32:28.443
which, of course, affected
life here in America as well,

00:32:30.390 --> 00:32:31.800
and Wittgenstein's advice

00:32:31.800 --> 00:32:34.440
was that such voluntary
work would be an antidote

00:32:34.440 --> 00:32:36.090
to the too-narrow confines

00:32:36.090 --> 00:32:38.940
of Drury's middle-class English society,

00:32:38.940 --> 00:32:41.070
which was still affected
by Victorian manners,

00:32:41.070 --> 00:32:42.243
very, very proper.

00:32:44.070 --> 00:32:45.900
Wittgenstein frequently expressed a view

00:32:45.900 --> 00:32:49.380
that teaching philosophy,
what he was doing himself,

00:32:49.380 --> 00:32:51.273
was a ridiculous profession,

00:32:52.290 --> 00:32:55.050
and so he was pleased when
Drury failed to be appointed

00:32:55.050 --> 00:32:57.150
to an academic post in philosophy

00:32:57.150 --> 00:32:59.670
when he applied for one about this time,

00:32:59.670 --> 00:33:01.713
and later, a little later,

00:33:01.713 --> 00:33:06.000
Wittgenstein intervened when Drury,

00:33:06.000 --> 00:33:08.700
who's still needing to settle on a career,

00:33:08.700 --> 00:33:11.040
was so moved by the
plight of a fellow worker

00:33:11.040 --> 00:33:13.830
who had suffered a mental breakdown

00:33:13.830 --> 00:33:15.000
that he wrote to Wittgenstein

00:33:15.000 --> 00:33:18.390
that he was considering
becoming a psychiatric nurse.

00:33:18.390 --> 00:33:19.920
Wittgenstein immediately suggested

00:33:19.920 --> 00:33:21.450
that in view of his level of education,

00:33:21.450 --> 00:33:25.860
Drury should instead
enroll in a medical school.

00:33:25.860 --> 00:33:28.350
Drury said, "But I don't have the money."

00:33:28.350 --> 00:33:31.230
Wittgenstein then went to
his friend Maynard Keynes,

00:33:31.230 --> 00:33:32.790
the famous economist,

00:33:32.790 --> 00:33:35.340
who spent every morning in bed until 12:00

00:33:35.340 --> 00:33:37.920
playing the stock market on the telephone,

00:33:37.920 --> 00:33:41.520
and Keynes came up with the cash.

00:33:41.520 --> 00:33:43.170
They then settled on the medical school

00:33:43.170 --> 00:33:46.080
at Trinity College in Dublin,

00:33:46.080 --> 00:33:49.860
a very old medical school founded in 1711,

00:33:49.860 --> 00:33:53.190
maybe slightly before
Yale itself was founded,

00:33:53.190 --> 00:33:56.640
and mainly on the basis

00:33:56.640 --> 00:34:00.210
that the fees there were
the cheapest they could find

00:34:00.210 --> 00:34:03.963
and Dublin, of course, was
Drury's father's native city.

00:34:05.370 --> 00:34:07.050
During the course of
Drury's medical studies,

00:34:07.050 --> 00:34:10.320
Wittgenstein visited him
Dublin several times,

00:34:10.320 --> 00:34:11.850
and I will return to an incident

00:34:11.850 --> 00:34:14.430
that happened during one
of these visits later on.

00:34:14.430 --> 00:34:16.680
At one stage, Wittgenstein asked Drury

00:34:16.680 --> 00:34:19.560
to inquire of his tutor
at Trinity College, Dublin

00:34:19.560 --> 00:34:22.560
whether Wittgenstein might join
him there to study medicine,

00:34:23.430 --> 00:34:24.870
a request that, not surprisingly,

00:34:24.870 --> 00:34:26.520
was greeted with astonishment.

00:34:26.520 --> 00:34:29.280
Be like a Yale professor

00:34:29.280 --> 00:34:33.150
applying to some medical school
in New York, Columbia maybe,

00:34:33.150 --> 00:34:34.717
and saying, "Can I join?

00:34:34.717 --> 00:34:36.870
"Can I become a doctor?"

00:34:36.870 --> 00:34:38.320
You know, it's extraordinary.

00:34:39.360 --> 00:34:40.320
The plan came to nothing,

00:34:40.320 --> 00:34:43.440
but then Wittgenstein also was
thinking of going to Russia

00:34:43.440 --> 00:34:48.033
and studying medicine, or
yeah, studying medicine there,

00:34:49.090 --> 00:34:51.960
and you have to understand
that in that case,

00:34:51.960 --> 00:34:56.490
in that environment in
Oxford-Cambridge in the 1930s,

00:34:56.490 --> 00:34:58.770
Marxism was very, very popular,

00:34:58.770 --> 00:35:02.070
and a lot of the people
there were communists

00:35:02.070 --> 00:35:04.530
and actually, eventually
proved to be spies

00:35:04.530 --> 00:35:05.930
during the Second World War,

00:35:07.620 --> 00:35:12.450
and they thought

00:35:12.450 --> 00:35:14.673
that Russia represented the future,

00:35:15.750 --> 00:35:17.583
socialist Russia.

00:35:21.990 --> 00:35:23.230
Here we are, (indistinct).

00:35:24.630 --> 00:35:26.220
If you could give, pass that down.

00:35:26.220 --> 00:35:28.420
Do you mind? Could you
pass it down, please?

00:35:30.150 --> 00:35:30.983
So

00:35:38.760 --> 00:35:39.593
anyway,

00:35:42.750 --> 00:35:46.230
Drury was quite happy with
the scientific studies

00:35:46.230 --> 00:35:48.480
and became first in his class,

00:35:48.480 --> 00:35:52.140
studying physiology and, you
know, biology and all that,

00:35:52.140 --> 00:35:54.750
but when he moved into the clinical phase

00:35:54.750 --> 00:35:59.043
of his medical education,

00:36:00.240 --> 00:36:03.393
he became, he suffered
a vocational crisis.

00:36:04.500 --> 00:36:07.380
I can guarantee that you're
gonna suffer a vocational crisis

00:36:07.380 --> 00:36:09.880
at some stage of your life,
maybe several of them.

00:36:10.860 --> 00:36:12.600
I can tell you that for nothing.

00:36:12.600 --> 00:36:15.810
I've been teaching students
your age for 40 years,

00:36:15.810 --> 00:36:17.670
and some of the most creative and best

00:36:17.670 --> 00:36:19.620
are the people that change their minds.

00:36:21.270 --> 00:36:25.560
Anyway, Drury was very despondent,
very down in the dumps,

00:36:25.560 --> 00:36:28.560
and Wittgenstein wrote him a letter,

00:36:28.560 --> 00:36:30.510
and this letter is the own...

00:36:30.510 --> 00:36:35.280
People, I archived all his writings,

00:36:35.280 --> 00:36:38.610
and everybody, I mean, they've
come from all over the world

00:36:38.610 --> 00:36:40.440
to Limerick, where I live,

00:36:40.440 --> 00:36:42.330
and they all expect that in the archive

00:36:42.330 --> 00:36:44.980
they're gonna find letters
that Wittgenstein wrote

00:36:46.830 --> 00:36:47.670
to Drury,

00:36:47.670 --> 00:36:50.370
and we know that he wrote many of them,

00:36:50.370 --> 00:36:52.440
and there's only one that survives,

00:36:52.440 --> 00:36:55.443
and I swear to God I haven't,
I'm not concealing anything.

00:36:56.760 --> 00:36:57.610
There's only one,

00:36:58.560 --> 00:37:02.887
and yeah, "Dear Drury, I
have thought a fair amount

00:37:02.887 --> 00:37:05.197
"about our conversation on Sunday,

00:37:05.197 --> 00:37:07.717
"and I would like to say,
or rather not to say,

00:37:07.717 --> 00:37:12.457
"but write a few things
about these conversations.

00:37:12.457 --> 00:37:14.407
"Mainly, I think this:

00:37:14.407 --> 00:37:18.067
"Don't think about yourself,
but think about others,

00:37:18.067 --> 00:37:20.467
"for example, your patients.

00:37:20.467 --> 00:37:22.080
"You said in the Phoenix Park,"

00:37:22.080 --> 00:37:25.620
that's the big park in
Dublin, "yesterday,"

00:37:25.620 --> 00:37:28.417
where they spent a lot of time walking,

00:37:28.417 --> 00:37:32.763
"that possibly you made a
mistake having taken up medicine.

00:37:33.817 --> 00:37:34.687
"You immediately added

00:37:34.687 --> 00:37:37.837
"that probably it was wrong
to think such a thing at all.

00:37:37.837 --> 00:37:41.587
"I am sure it is, but not
because being a doctor,

00:37:41.587 --> 00:37:44.527
"you may not, not because being a doctor

00:37:44.527 --> 00:37:48.727
"you may not go the wrong
way or go to the dogs,

00:37:48.727 --> 00:37:49.987
"but because if you do,

00:37:49.987 --> 00:37:51.577
"this has nothing to do

00:37:51.577 --> 00:37:55.147
"with your choice of a
profession being a mistake,

00:37:55.147 --> 00:37:57.397
"for what human being can say

00:37:57.397 --> 00:37:59.017
"what would have been the right thing

00:37:59.017 --> 00:38:00.333
"if this is the wrong one?

00:38:01.387 --> 00:38:02.617
"You didn't make a mistake

00:38:02.617 --> 00:38:03.937
"because there was nothing at the time

00:38:03.937 --> 00:38:06.907
"you knew or ought to have
known that you overlooked.

00:38:06.907 --> 00:38:08.647
"You did your best.

00:38:08.647 --> 00:38:13.087
"Only this could have been
called making a mistake,

00:38:13.087 --> 00:38:15.817
"and even if you made a
mistake in this sense,

00:38:15.817 --> 00:38:18.877
"this would now have to
be regarded as datum,

00:38:18.877 --> 00:38:21.817
"as all the other circumstances
inside and outside,

00:38:21.817 --> 00:38:23.223
"which you can't alter.

00:38:25.387 --> 00:38:29.647
"The thing now is to live in
the world in which you are,

00:38:29.647 --> 00:38:32.107
"not to think of or dream about

00:38:32.107 --> 00:38:34.863
"the world you would like to be in.

00:38:36.457 --> 00:38:40.807
"Look at people's sufferings,
physical and mental.

00:38:40.807 --> 00:38:43.057
"You have them close at hand,

00:38:43.057 --> 00:38:46.777
"and this ought to be a good
remedy for your troubles.

00:38:46.777 --> 00:38:49.027
"Another way is to take a rest

00:38:49.027 --> 00:38:52.657
"whenever you ought to take
one and collect yourself.

00:38:52.657 --> 00:38:55.447
"Not with me because I wouldn't rest you.

00:38:55.447 --> 00:38:56.737
"As for religious thoughts,

00:38:56.737 --> 00:39:01.627
"I do not think the craving
for placidity is religious,

00:39:01.627 --> 00:39:03.573
"which is often how religion is sold:

00:39:04.567 --> 00:39:07.717
"something one ought to hunt after.

00:39:07.717 --> 00:39:09.307
"Look at your patients more closely

00:39:09.307 --> 00:39:11.197
"as human beings in trouble,

00:39:11.197 --> 00:39:13.207
"and enjoy more the opportunity you have

00:39:13.207 --> 00:39:16.477
"to say 'goodnight' to many people.

00:39:16.477 --> 00:39:18.967
"This alone is a gift from heaven,

00:39:18.967 --> 00:39:22.117
"which many people would envy you,

00:39:22.117 --> 00:39:24.370
"and this sort of thing ought to heal you

00:39:25.717 --> 00:39:28.897
"and heal your frail soul, I believe.

00:39:28.897 --> 00:39:31.627
"It won't rest it, but when
you are healthily tired,

00:39:31.627 --> 00:39:34.117
"you may, you can just take a rest.

00:39:34.117 --> 00:39:37.297
"I think, in some sense, you
don't look at people's face,

00:39:37.297 --> 00:39:40.237
"the window of the soul, closely enough.

00:39:40.237 --> 00:39:41.497
"In conversations with me,

00:39:41.497 --> 00:39:44.047
"don't so much try to
have the conversations

00:39:44.047 --> 00:39:46.327
"which you think would taste well,

00:39:46.327 --> 00:39:48.307
"though you will never get that anyway,

00:39:48.307 --> 00:39:49.837
"but try to have the conversations

00:39:49.837 --> 00:39:52.867
"which will have the
pleasantest aftertaste.

00:39:52.867 --> 00:39:54.247
"It is most important

00:39:54.247 --> 00:39:56.887
"that we should not one
day have to tell ourselves

00:39:56.887 --> 00:39:58.417
"that we had wasted the time

00:39:58.417 --> 00:40:00.787
"we were allowed to spend together.

00:40:00.787 --> 00:40:05.217
"I wish you good thoughts,
but chiefly, good feelings."

00:40:06.150 --> 00:40:07.830
For my part, I see many echoes here

00:40:07.830 --> 00:40:10.440
of Plato's contrast between medical care,

00:40:10.440 --> 00:40:11.730
worthy of free man,

00:40:11.730 --> 00:40:14.790
and his implied criticism
of the kind of doctor

00:40:14.790 --> 00:40:16.530
who never gives any account

00:40:16.530 --> 00:40:19.710
of the particular illness
of the individual slave

00:40:19.710 --> 00:40:21.990
or is prepared to listen to one,

00:40:21.990 --> 00:40:24.120
but simply prescribes

00:40:24.120 --> 00:40:28.140
what he thinks best in
the light of experience

00:40:28.140 --> 00:40:30.630
as if he had precise knowledge

00:40:30.630 --> 00:40:32.910
and with the self-confidence
of a dictator.

00:40:32.910 --> 00:40:34.923
That's the end of the quote from Plato.

00:40:36.690 --> 00:40:39.480
Immediately after the
war, after the graduating,

00:40:39.480 --> 00:40:44.480
Drury joined the Royal Army Medical Corps,

00:40:44.580 --> 00:40:48.090
and he had a pretty rough time.

00:40:48.090 --> 00:40:51.840
He had a easy time in Egypt, in Cairo,

00:40:51.840 --> 00:40:55.443
but afterwards, he followed,
in a tented hospital,

00:40:57.818 --> 00:40:59.403
the advance of the army.

00:41:00.960 --> 00:41:02.640
He was landed on the beaches at Normandy

00:41:02.640 --> 00:41:07.320
and then followed the advance
of the army, the British Army,

00:41:07.320 --> 00:41:09.960
across Northern France

00:41:09.960 --> 00:41:12.600
and into Holland,

00:41:12.600 --> 00:41:14.520
and then the Battle of the Bulge,

00:41:14.520 --> 00:41:17.520
in which American soldiers
distinguished themselves greatly,

00:41:18.420 --> 00:41:21.360
and he was in a tented hospital,

00:41:21.360 --> 00:41:24.033
and it was rough going.

00:41:25.230 --> 00:41:27.423
He returned to Dublin after the war,

00:41:28.500 --> 00:41:30.870
after five years of service.

00:41:30.870 --> 00:41:34.680
During the war,
Wittgenstein himself worked,

00:41:34.680 --> 00:41:37.170
even though he was professor
of philosophy in Cambridge,

00:41:37.170 --> 00:41:39.960
he went up there a day or two a week,

00:41:39.960 --> 00:41:43.020
but for the rest, he worked
in Guy's Hospital in London

00:41:43.020 --> 00:41:47.100
as a porter

00:41:47.100 --> 00:41:48.420
and a pharmaceutical assistant,

00:41:48.420 --> 00:41:51.420
which meant he went 'round the
wards bringing the medicines,

00:41:51.420 --> 00:41:52.440
and then he got involved

00:41:52.440 --> 00:41:56.043
in a research project regarding shock,

00:41:58.350 --> 00:42:00.810
shock that came to people

00:42:00.810 --> 00:42:05.490
after they had the
bombing in London, really,

00:42:05.490 --> 00:42:08.670
I mean, I can think of the same
thing in Kiev at the moment,

00:42:08.670 --> 00:42:10.350
and Ukraine generally, you know,

00:42:10.350 --> 00:42:14.910
it does leave huge marks on the
people who have been bombed,

00:42:14.910 --> 00:42:19.650
and anyway, he never really
settled in philosophy

00:42:19.650 --> 00:42:20.670
after that point,

00:42:20.670 --> 00:42:23.280
and eventually, he decided that he might,

00:42:23.280 --> 00:42:24.180
there was a book in him

00:42:24.180 --> 00:42:26.700
that he wanted to write in the 1930s,

00:42:26.700 --> 00:42:28.350
the book which he never finished,

00:42:28.350 --> 00:42:30.300
but was published posthumously

00:42:30.300 --> 00:42:32.310
as the "Philosophical Investigations."

00:42:32.310 --> 00:42:34.083
This is Wittgenstein,

00:42:35.190 --> 00:42:37.263
and so there were two men in crisis,

00:42:40.080 --> 00:42:44.310
and eventually, Drury
secured a six-month contract

00:42:44.310 --> 00:42:49.020
in St. Patrick's Psychiatric Hospital

00:42:49.020 --> 00:42:49.853
in Dublin,

00:42:49.853 --> 00:42:52.200
which was actually
founded by Jonathan Swift

00:42:52.200 --> 00:42:55.320
the writer of "Gulliver's Travels,"

00:42:55.320 --> 00:42:57.870
and he actually stuck with psychiatry

00:42:57.870 --> 00:43:02.220
and stayed there until he retired

00:43:02.220 --> 00:43:05.160
with cardiac-related symptoms.

00:43:05.160 --> 00:43:07.320
He died on Christmas day in 1976.

00:43:07.320 --> 00:43:09.303
That's Drury,

00:43:10.800 --> 00:43:13.837
so Wittgenstein, after retiring, decided,

00:43:13.837 --> 00:43:17.767
"Will I go to Norway," where
he had a hut on a fjord,

00:43:17.767 --> 00:43:19.380
"or will I go to Ireland?"

00:43:19.380 --> 00:43:21.030
He came back to Ireland,

00:43:21.030 --> 00:43:23.220
and while there,

00:43:23.220 --> 00:43:26.760
he got a very, very severe mental,

00:43:26.760 --> 00:43:29.670
he became very severely mentally ill,

00:43:29.670 --> 00:43:34.503
and he was referred by
Drury to Drury's boss,

00:43:35.790 --> 00:43:37.143
Professor Norman Moore.

00:43:38.520 --> 00:43:40.200
Again, we don't know an awful lot

00:43:40.200 --> 00:43:42.360
about what the problem was,

00:43:42.360 --> 00:43:43.680
but eventually,

00:43:43.680 --> 00:43:47.613
he went to the wildest
part of Ireland, Connemara,

00:43:48.540 --> 00:43:53.163
and to a cottage that Drury's
elder brother had there.

00:43:54.810 --> 00:43:57.430
Wittgenstein died of prostatic cancer

00:43:59.040 --> 00:44:00.790
in 1951

00:44:01.830 --> 00:44:04.230
in the home of a medical
friend of Drury's,

00:44:04.230 --> 00:44:06.303
Dr. Bevan in Cambridge.

00:44:07.890 --> 00:44:10.980
Now, at the beginning,
I quoted from Plato,

00:44:10.980 --> 00:44:12.840
and now I want to quote from Drury

00:44:12.840 --> 00:44:17.840
about an event which took
place a good deal earlier

00:44:18.000 --> 00:44:20.073
in Dublin in 1936.

00:44:21.510 --> 00:44:23.077
Drury recalled,

00:44:23.077 --> 00:44:25.717
"When I was a medical
student here in Dublin,

00:44:25.717 --> 00:44:28.897
"and Wittgenstein came over to visit me,

00:44:28.897 --> 00:44:33.067
"he wanted to give talks
with some mental patients.

00:44:33.067 --> 00:44:35.370
"I arranged for him to go
to St. Patrick's Hospital,"

00:44:35.370 --> 00:44:37.087
that's the psychiatric hospital,

00:44:37.087 --> 00:44:39.997
"as a visit whenever he wanted to.

00:44:39.997 --> 00:44:41.197
"There was one old man

00:44:41.197 --> 00:44:43.623
"who had been in the
hospital for many years,

00:44:44.467 --> 00:44:46.977
"and Wittgenstein said about him,

00:44:47.982 --> 00:44:50.432
"'He was much more
intelligent that his doctors.'

00:44:52.537 --> 00:44:54.690
"This man had read a
lot of Herbert Spencer,"

00:44:54.690 --> 00:44:57.963
that's the evolutionary theorist,

00:44:58.987 --> 00:45:00.003
"and one day,

00:45:01.087 --> 00:45:02.977
"when he was having a
discussion with Wittgenstein,

00:45:02.977 --> 00:45:04.807
"I was there too,

00:45:04.807 --> 00:45:07.147
"and I attempted to join in

00:45:07.147 --> 00:45:10.260
"with some rather shallow
logical positivism,"

00:45:10.260 --> 00:45:12.490
which was the then-fashionable philosophy

00:45:13.350 --> 00:45:16.683
in England (indistinct)
to a book by A.J. Ayer,

00:45:18.480 --> 00:45:23.130
which actually summarized
trends in philosophy

00:45:23.130 --> 00:45:28.130
which had developed in Vienna,
Wittgenstein's home city,

00:45:28.380 --> 00:45:30.177
and very much influenced by Wittgenstein,

00:45:30.177 --> 00:45:32.850
but Wittgenstein never, ever agreed

00:45:32.850 --> 00:45:35.103
that anybody could speak for him,

00:45:36.607 --> 00:45:40.620
"so Wittgenstein told me to
shut up when he intervened,"

00:45:40.620 --> 00:45:44.827
when Drury intervened in this
discussion with the patient.

00:45:44.827 --> 00:45:46.897
"Later, when he was talking
about it on our own,

00:45:46.897 --> 00:45:50.437
"he said, 'When you are playing ping-pong,

00:45:50.437 --> 00:45:52.337
"'you shouldn't use a tennis racket.'"

00:45:53.640 --> 00:45:55.650
In other words, be sensitive.

00:45:55.650 --> 00:45:59.043
Now, amazingly, Albert Camus,
have you ever heard of him?

00:45:59.910 --> 00:46:02.490
A French writer, a philosopher,

00:46:02.490 --> 00:46:05.730
extremely interesting man as well.

00:46:05.730 --> 00:46:07.383
He tells a very similar story,

00:46:08.370 --> 00:46:10.143
but has an interesting twist in it.

00:46:12.193 --> 00:46:13.083
I got it here.

00:46:18.060 --> 00:46:20.677
He said, "In a psychiatric hospital,

00:46:20.677 --> 00:46:23.947
"a passing doctor noticed
one of his patients

00:46:23.947 --> 00:46:27.330
"fishing with a rod in a bathtub."

00:46:27.330 --> 00:46:28.163
Imagine it.

00:46:29.467 --> 00:46:30.397
"As he walked by,

00:46:30.397 --> 00:46:34.177
"mindful of this indispensable partnership

00:46:34.177 --> 00:46:36.517
"between two human beings,

00:46:36.517 --> 00:46:41.400
"the doctor asked the
patient, 'Are they biting?'"

00:46:41.400 --> 00:46:44.497
the fish in the bathtub, (chuckles)

00:46:44.497 --> 00:46:46.803
"to which the patient replied, 'Idiot!

00:46:48.029 --> 00:46:53.029
"'Can't you even see that it
is a bathtub?'" (chuckles)

00:46:53.100 --> 00:46:56.803
So yeah, (laughs)

00:46:58.470 --> 00:47:01.650
Now, I have stressed very much

00:47:01.650 --> 00:47:05.530
that this all depends on
the store of experience

00:47:06.510 --> 00:47:11.510
and the examined life that a
doctor brings to his patients,

00:47:13.560 --> 00:47:16.830
and by experience, I
think I can't do better

00:47:16.830 --> 00:47:21.360
than quote from one of
Wittgenstein's favorite novelists,

00:47:21.360 --> 00:47:25.767
Dostoevsky in "The Brothers Karamazov."

00:47:26.647 --> 00:47:29.830
"It was said by many people
about the elder Zossima

00:47:30.697 --> 00:47:33.787
"that by permitting
everyone for so many years

00:47:33.787 --> 00:47:37.207
"to come to bare their souls and hearts

00:47:37.207 --> 00:47:40.957
"and beg his advice and healing words,

00:47:40.957 --> 00:47:42.157
"he had absorbed

00:47:42.157 --> 00:47:47.157
"so many secrets, sorrows,
and avowals into his soul

00:47:47.647 --> 00:47:51.097
"that in the end, he had
acquired so fine a perception

00:47:51.097 --> 00:47:52.807
"that he could tell at the first glance

00:47:52.807 --> 00:47:55.147
"from the face of a stranger

00:47:55.147 --> 00:47:58.897
"what he had come for, what he wanted,

00:47:58.897 --> 00:48:01.857
"and what kind of torment
wracked his conscience."

00:48:02.850 --> 00:48:04.237
Wittgenstein assured Drury,

00:48:04.237 --> 00:48:07.473
"Yes, there really have
been people like that,

00:48:08.497 --> 00:48:10.447
"and indeed, I believe that our doctors

00:48:10.447 --> 00:48:12.217
"and other health professionals

00:48:12.217 --> 00:48:15.997
"who can read their patients,
as it were, at a glance,

00:48:15.997 --> 00:48:17.857
"but unfortunately, this is a facility

00:48:17.857 --> 00:48:22.383
"which is feigned in the reality,

00:48:23.647 --> 00:48:26.107
"and it's absented in the slave doctor

00:48:26.107 --> 00:48:28.057
"who briskly proceeds from bed to bed

00:48:28.057 --> 00:48:31.680
"and pretends he can read
the patient like that,"

00:48:31.680 --> 00:48:33.380
but there are people who can read,

00:48:34.530 --> 00:48:37.590
can zoom in straightaway.

00:48:37.590 --> 00:48:40.860
Now, will I go on

00:48:40.860 --> 00:48:43.440
for just a few more minutes?

00:48:43.440 --> 00:48:44.520
I mentioned earlier

00:48:44.520 --> 00:48:47.190
that Drury collected, in
"The Danger of Words,"

00:48:47.190 --> 00:48:49.470
a number of papers he'd originally given,

00:48:49.470 --> 00:48:51.333
mainly to groups of doctors,

00:48:52.290 --> 00:48:54.240
as an illustration of the influence

00:48:54.240 --> 00:48:56.613
that Wittgenstein had on him.

00:48:57.817 --> 00:49:00.607
"I notice here references to problems,

00:49:00.607 --> 00:49:02.670
"practical difficulty, and perplexity."

00:49:02.670 --> 00:49:04.620
I haven't given you the full quotation.

00:49:05.737 --> 00:49:07.627
"By way of introduction to another facet

00:49:07.627 --> 00:49:10.057
"of the relationship between
philosophy and medicine

00:49:10.057 --> 00:49:11.647
"that is much more problematic

00:49:11.647 --> 00:49:13.807
"than we have discussed so far.

00:49:13.807 --> 00:49:15.067
"That concerns the role

00:49:15.067 --> 00:49:17.947
"of scientific investigation in medicine,

00:49:17.947 --> 00:49:22.147
"and that, too, had definite,
albeit not exclusive, origins

00:49:22.147 --> 00:49:24.393
"in certain philosophical trends.

00:49:25.537 --> 00:49:29.650
"It's because of the
philosophy of the physics

00:49:31.687 --> 00:49:35.677
"that where they decided

00:49:35.677 --> 00:49:39.430
"that there were four

00:49:43.117 --> 00:49:44.347
"elementary subjects,

00:49:44.347 --> 00:49:46.537
"substances that go to make up the world:

00:49:46.537 --> 00:49:47.987
"earth, air, fire, and water.

00:49:49.057 --> 00:49:52.177
"This was reflected in the body

00:49:52.177 --> 00:49:55.357
"in four elementary bodily fluids:

00:49:55.357 --> 00:49:58.323
"phlegm, yellow bile,
black bile, and blood,

00:49:59.317 --> 00:50:01.887
"all in a dynamic balance,"

00:50:02.910 --> 00:50:05.190
and well into the 19th century,

00:50:05.190 --> 00:50:08.790
the medical practice of
bloodletting was justified

00:50:08.790 --> 00:50:13.290
on the pretext of restoring
the bodily fluids,

00:50:13.290 --> 00:50:16.380
that if you had kind of a,

00:50:16.380 --> 00:50:19.233
I'm putting a flushed face, you should,

00:50:22.200 --> 00:50:26.583
some blood would be drawn
by leeches, little animals,

00:50:28.177 --> 00:50:31.807
"so this was actually in contravention

00:50:31.807 --> 00:50:36.187
"of what the Greek idea of medicine was,

00:50:36.187 --> 00:50:37.717
"which was that one should rely

00:50:37.717 --> 00:50:40.773
"mainly on the natural
powers of the body to heal,

00:50:43.117 --> 00:50:44.887
"and as is obvious,

00:50:44.887 --> 00:50:48.427
"this power lies outside the
direct control of the doctor.

00:50:48.427 --> 00:50:51.337
"The Greek philosophers saw
similarity in that respect

00:50:51.337 --> 00:50:53.403
"with their own art, which was teaching.

00:50:55.597 --> 00:50:56.917
"At the last resort,

00:50:56.917 --> 00:51:00.187
"the power to learn does
not lie with the teacher,

00:51:00.187 --> 00:51:03.180
"no more than the power
to heal with the doctor."

00:51:03.180 --> 00:51:06.483
I can't make you learn anything, you know?

00:51:08.715 --> 00:51:09.603
But you do learn.

00:51:11.250 --> 00:51:13.050
You learn because you want to learn,

00:51:15.990 --> 00:51:19.500
and in a certain sense,
what they were saying is

00:51:19.500 --> 00:51:21.480
that you can rely on the body to heal,

00:51:21.480 --> 00:51:24.753
and just so I can rely on
your intelligence to learn,

00:51:26.220 --> 00:51:29.940
and nature is the primary healer,

00:51:29.940 --> 00:51:31.290
at least that was the Greek

00:51:35.640 --> 00:51:36.900
conviction,

00:51:36.900 --> 00:51:40.800
and a very important German
philosopher of medicine

00:51:40.800 --> 00:51:43.290
called Hans-Georg Gadamer,

00:51:43.290 --> 00:51:47.073
characterized healing as
enigmatic on that account,

00:51:48.210 --> 00:51:51.330
and perhaps we can let fly the cinnamons,

00:51:51.330 --> 00:51:54.273
difficult to interpret, mysterious even,

00:51:55.500 --> 00:51:58.680
to amplify what Drury was getting at.

00:51:58.680 --> 00:52:00.810
Now, I was going to
talk to you a little bit

00:52:00.810 --> 00:52:02.880
about interventionist medicine

00:52:02.880 --> 00:52:07.620
and trace it back to Francis Bacon

00:52:07.620 --> 00:52:11.850
and also to Descartes,

00:52:11.850 --> 00:52:13.800
but I think you've probably
heard a lot about that,

00:52:13.800 --> 00:52:15.847
but basically, these guys said,

00:52:15.847 --> 00:52:18.468
"Intervene, intervene, intervene.

00:52:18.468 --> 00:52:22.710
"Get involved with...

00:52:22.710 --> 00:52:26.167
They all admitted that the
current science got you nowhere.

00:52:26.167 --> 00:52:28.027
"But this,

00:52:28.027 --> 00:52:32.107
"it had a huge promissory value,

00:52:32.107 --> 00:52:34.537
"and health would become the foundation

00:52:34.537 --> 00:52:38.670
"of all the other blessings
in this life," said Bacon,

00:52:38.670 --> 00:52:40.380
and what they were looking for was

00:52:40.380 --> 00:52:42.990
a cure for incurable diseases,

00:52:42.990 --> 00:52:45.750
the enhancement of human capacity,

00:52:45.750 --> 00:52:47.463
the prolonging of life, even.

00:52:48.360 --> 00:52:50.973
You know, these are still
things where we're at,

00:52:53.730 --> 00:52:57.240
and these promissory notes

00:52:57.240 --> 00:53:00.540
were taken up over time

00:53:00.540 --> 00:53:03.540
in the universities,

00:53:03.540 --> 00:53:07.110
whereas now, I think,
the medical faculties

00:53:07.110 --> 00:53:09.960
have a primacy far, far greater

00:53:09.960 --> 00:53:12.063
than the arts and humanities faculties.

00:53:13.710 --> 00:53:17.193
You know, that's how it's,
that's what's happened.

00:53:19.740 --> 00:53:24.740
Now, this kind of philosophical reflection

00:53:25.860 --> 00:53:29.643
privileging science has had
many consequences for medicine.

00:53:30.750 --> 00:53:32.670
The doctor or medical researcher

00:53:32.670 --> 00:53:35.073
are portrayed as dealing
with the body only,

00:53:35.940 --> 00:53:38.430
often modeled on a machine

00:53:38.430 --> 00:53:42.060
such that machine is an
extension of engineering

00:53:42.060 --> 00:53:44.340
with the brain modeled on the computer,

00:53:44.340 --> 00:53:45.813
software to hardware,

00:53:47.370 --> 00:53:51.180
but there's always the feeling
there's something more,

00:53:51.180 --> 00:53:54.540
and more that features, inescapably,

00:53:54.540 --> 00:53:56.910
in the interpersonal negotiation

00:53:56.910 --> 00:54:00.303
that is the ancient and
venerable medical interview.

00:54:01.620 --> 00:54:06.620
Wittgenstein entered into this debate,

00:54:07.170 --> 00:54:09.510
and in his earlier book,

00:54:09.510 --> 00:54:11.767
he ends with, "Whereof one cannot speak,

00:54:11.767 --> 00:54:13.897
"therefore one must be silent.

00:54:13.897 --> 00:54:16.237
"You can speak science,

00:54:16.237 --> 00:54:20.467
"but the other things,

00:54:20.467 --> 00:54:22.837
"about which you cannot speak,

00:54:22.837 --> 00:54:27.037
"the appreciation of beauty in aesthetics,

00:54:27.037 --> 00:54:28.717
"the values that ought to be exemplified

00:54:28.717 --> 00:54:31.417
"in one's behavior in ethics,

00:54:31.417 --> 00:54:35.707
"the wonder at the very existence
of the world, mysticism,

00:54:35.707 --> 00:54:38.557
"in these cases, all you can do," he said,

00:54:38.557 --> 00:54:43.020
"at that point, is look and point."

00:54:43.020 --> 00:54:45.450
Look at that. Feel that.

00:54:45.450 --> 00:54:46.323
That matters.

00:54:48.330 --> 00:54:50.580
Later on, he modified that somewhat

00:54:50.580 --> 00:54:53.010
with the notion of language games,

00:54:53.010 --> 00:54:54.660
but he was against,

00:54:54.660 --> 00:54:57.240
which we've discussed last day, scientism.

00:54:57.240 --> 00:54:58.443
I'm coming to an end.

00:55:01.260 --> 00:55:05.700
I'm sure he was influenced in
his own criticism of science,

00:55:05.700 --> 00:55:08.200
and remember, he started
out like was an engineer.

00:55:10.740 --> 00:55:12.870
By his own experience of
the mechanized slaughter

00:55:12.870 --> 00:55:15.450
in the trenches of the First World War,

00:55:15.450 --> 00:55:19.110
the industrial-scale
extermination of the Jews,

00:55:19.110 --> 00:55:22.770
a heritage he could claim
as his own under the Nazis,

00:55:22.770 --> 00:55:24.270
and after 1945,

00:55:24.270 --> 00:55:27.010
the nuclear threat that
still hangs over mankind

00:55:28.020 --> 00:55:29.043
with Mr. Putin,

00:55:30.420 --> 00:55:34.590
and all these are examples
of the abuse of science

00:55:34.590 --> 00:55:36.513
unforeseen by Descartes or Bacon,

00:55:37.770 --> 00:55:41.670
so put it this way:

00:55:41.670 --> 00:55:44.370
Wittgenstein, and Drury picked this up,

00:55:44.370 --> 00:55:46.470
was an anti-Cartesian,

00:55:46.470 --> 00:55:50.610
anti- seeing a huge separation

00:55:50.610 --> 00:55:52.560
between mind and body.

00:55:52.560 --> 00:55:55.023
We are embodied beings,

00:55:56.940 --> 00:56:00.960
and ne'er can the two be separated,

00:56:00.960 --> 00:56:02.760
and they're separated only,

00:56:02.760 --> 00:56:06.330
at their separation gives
an entree into science

00:56:06.330 --> 00:56:09.153
into areas where it
shouldn't go and can't go.

00:56:11.130 --> 00:56:13.050
Nevertheless, Drury,

00:56:13.050 --> 00:56:16.233
and with Wittgenstein's approval,

00:56:17.820 --> 00:56:21.870
were welcomed the use of
pharmacology in psychiatry,

00:56:21.870 --> 00:56:26.283
which happened in the 1950s, I suppose,

00:56:27.630 --> 00:56:31.387
but he, Wittgenstein, warned,

00:56:31.387 --> 00:56:33.097
"Don't let yourself think

00:56:33.097 --> 00:56:35.587
"that all human problems
can be solved in this way.

00:56:35.587 --> 00:56:36.420
"They can't.

00:56:39.007 --> 00:56:41.497
"Although our intelligence
and our willpower

00:56:41.497 --> 00:56:43.477
"are dependent on the proper functioning

00:56:43.477 --> 00:56:46.417
"of a very complicated and delicate

00:56:46.417 --> 00:56:49.297
"neurohumoral mechanism

00:56:49.297 --> 00:56:51.667
"over which we have no control,

00:56:51.667 --> 00:56:54.573
"our sanity is at the mercy of a molecule,

00:56:55.927 --> 00:56:58.207
"but that does not mean

00:56:58.207 --> 00:57:02.247
"that all the answers lie
on that level of analysis."

00:57:04.350 --> 00:57:08.550
In mental illness, Drury stressed

00:57:08.550 --> 00:57:10.680
the terrifying loneliness

00:57:10.680 --> 00:57:12.723
of the experience of mental illness,

00:57:14.010 --> 00:57:15.127
but he said, "A loneliness

00:57:15.127 --> 00:57:17.947
"that can make the sufferer more aware

00:57:17.947 --> 00:57:20.583
"of the mysteriousness
of our present being.

00:57:21.937 --> 00:57:25.177
"Indeed, the patient may
thereby gain insights

00:57:25.177 --> 00:57:27.837
"not yet appreciated
by his medical carer."

00:57:28.710 --> 00:57:30.780
As Wittgenstein remarked

00:57:30.780 --> 00:57:33.877
to the patient in St. Patrick's Hospital,

00:57:33.877 --> 00:57:36.367
"I think this man is much more intelligent

00:57:36.367 --> 00:57:37.797
"than any of his doctors."

00:57:38.910 --> 00:57:41.010
In sum, mental illness

00:57:41.010 --> 00:57:45.060
can make the enigma of
each person apparent,

00:57:45.060 --> 00:57:46.980
not only to the sufferer,

00:57:46.980 --> 00:57:49.020
but for those who care for him or her,

00:57:49.020 --> 00:57:52.170
especially, perhaps, the medical carer,

00:57:52.170 --> 00:57:54.270
and it is because of that enigma

00:57:54.270 --> 00:57:57.870
with its attendant uncertainty

00:57:57.870 --> 00:57:59.700
that medical practice continues to be

00:57:59.700 --> 00:58:03.300
as much an art as it is a science.

00:58:03.300 --> 00:58:07.440
This art can only be mastered
by an onerous apprenticeship

00:58:07.440 --> 00:58:09.660
such as Drury experienced.

00:58:09.660 --> 00:58:11.280
Through that apprenticeship,

00:58:11.280 --> 00:58:14.520
the doctor learns when to intervene

00:58:14.520 --> 00:58:17.160
using valuable scientific resources,

00:58:17.160 --> 00:58:21.180
and when to let nature
take its healing course.

00:58:21.180 --> 00:58:24.750
This discrimination is
especially important

00:58:24.750 --> 00:58:26.250
when the doctor faces

00:58:26.250 --> 00:58:30.180
his or her last task,

00:58:30.180 --> 00:58:33.250
the palliative one of
conducting the patient

00:58:34.170 --> 00:58:37.383
to the gates of the unknown at our death.

00:58:39.150 --> 00:58:40.689
Thank you.

00:58:40.689 --> 00:58:43.689
(audience clapping)

00:58:46.663 --> 00:58:49.413
(ethereal music)

